The apparatus shown in Fig. 8.1 is used in an experiment to find the magnetic flux density $B$ between the poles of a horseshoe magnet. Assume the magnetic field is uniform between the poles of the magnet and zero elsewhere.

{{figure:figure_8_1}}

The rigid metal rod of length $300\text{ mm}$ is fixed in position perpendicular to the direction of the magnetic field. The poles of the magnet are both $45\text{ mm}$ long. There is a current in the rod that causes a force on the rod. The balance is used to determine the magnitude of the force.

The variation with current $I$ of the force $F$ on the rod is shown in Fig. 8.2.

{{figure:figure_8_2}}

Calculate the magnetic flux density $B$.

